csv:     figure-3-drag-coefficient.csv
source:  ../source.pdf p.33 = report page 23, the figures section
anchor:  Figure 3  Drag Coefficient of Fragments
anchor:  supersonic value of 1.28

# PROVENANCE. Not typed by hand and not read by eye: produced by
# experiment/fragmentation-field/challenges/source-data-audit/checks/
# dod-1975-figure-3-trace.py, which traces the curve's black stroke off a
# 300-dpi render of the retained scan and emits this file with --write. Columns
# are the stroke's midpoint (cd) and its two edges (cd_lo, cd_hi); the stroke
# widens where the curve is steep, so cd_lo..cd_hi is the honest read band, not
# an error bar on the source.
#
# SUPERSEDES ../figure-3-digitized.md, which is an eyeballed reading and is
# WRONG through the transonic rise -- see that file's FINDING marker and
# ledger s13. Consumers must read this CSV. The one hand-copied array of the
# old table is in updates/mach-dependent-fragment-drag/checks/
# required-retardation-vs-mach.py:29 and is the repair the finding tracks.
#
# CLOSURE. The source states its own supersonic value in the text on pdf p.18
# ("take the drag coefficient as constant at its supersonic value of 1.28"),
# so the plateau is a definitional check, not a plausibility judgement: every
# row above Mach 3 must reproduce 1.28. The subsonic plateau and the peak have
# no stated value in the text; they are pinned here at what the page shows,
# which is also what card.md reports, so the three of them together fix the
# axis calibration in both directions. A calibration error large enough to
# matter cannot leave all three intact.
row:  (cd if mach >= 3.0 else 1.28) == 1.28 within 0.01
row:  (cd if mach <= 0.45 else 1.08) == 1.08 within 0.01
row:  (cd if 1.40 <= mach <= 1.55 else 1.40) == 1.40 within 0.02

# The three pins above all sit on flat segments, so a single-nearest-column
# misread on the steep transonic rise (or the smaller wiggle just past the
# plateau) can pass all three while still being wrong -- exactly what
# happened at mach=1.00 (traced 1.233, page ~1.257, fixed 2026-08-08) and
# mach=2.60 (traced 1.280, page ~1.294, fixed 2026-08-08). Pin both directly,
# cross-checked against three independent PNG re-traces
# (dod-1975-figure-3-mach1-gridline-probe.py, -independent-trace.py,
# -csv-page-residual.py) rather than the source.pdf render this table's own
# producing script uses -- the retained source.pdf is not present in every
# checkout (gitignored), so this invariant is the durable record even when
# the PDF itself is absent.
row:  (cd if mach == 1.00 else 1.257) == 1.257 within 0.01
row:  (cd if mach == 2.60 else 1.294) == 1.294 within 0.01

# The curve never leaves the printed axis box, and the midpoint always lies
# between the stroke edges it was computed from.
row:  (0 if 1.07 <= cd <= 1.41 else 1) == 0 within 0.5
row:  (0 if cd_lo <= cd <= cd_hi else 1) == 0 within 0.5

monotonic:  mach increasing
